{id:"citizen-lab",passage:"When the coastal town of Mereford invited residents to measure air quality, officials expected enthusiasm to fade after a few weekends. Instead, volunteers began questioning why sensors had been placed beside main roads but not near the harbour, where diesel engines ran throughout the night. Their challenge initially irritated the research team, who feared that changing the design would make earlier readings difficult to compare. A compromise was eventually reached: the original sensors remained, while a second set was added at locations chosen by residents. The new data did not overturn the first results, but it revealed short peaks that daily averages had concealed. More importantly, the project changed the relationship between experts and volunteers. Participants stopped seeing themselves as unpaid assistants and began to understand how decisions about measurement shape the story data can tell.",questions:[["What is the passage mainly concerned with?","Main idea",["Why volunteers cannot collect reliable data","How public participation improved both a study and its assumptions","Why harbour pollution is always underestimated","How officials replaced professional researchers"],"b","The project improved when residents questioned its design."],["Why were researchers reluctant to move the original sensors?","Detail",["The harbour was inaccessible","They wanted comparable results","Residents lacked permission","The equipment measured only traffic"],"b","Changing the original design threatened comparability."],["What did the additional sensors reveal?","Inference",["The first readings were fabricated","Brief pollution events were hidden by averages","Road traffic produced no pollution","Diesel engines ran only by day"],"b","Short peaks had disappeared inside daily averages."],["In context, ‘shape the story’ means","Vocabulary in context",["make the report entertaining","influence the interpretation produced by evidence","change measurements dishonestly","turn data into fiction"],"b","Measurement choices affect what conclusions data supports."],["The writer’s attitude to the compromise is","Author's attitude",["cautiously approving","openly hostile","indifferent","nostalgic"],"a","The benefits are stressed without claiming perfection."]]},
{id:"ice-library",passage:"In 1897, the crew of a weather station in the Arctic received crates of books selected by a committee thousands of kilometres away. The committee sent practical manuals, moral essays and several heavy histories, assuming that isolated scientists would want serious instruction. Crew diaries tell a different story. The manuals were useful, but the most frequently repaired volumes were comic novels and collections of travel tales. Readers annotated margins, argued over endings and occasionally performed scenes during the polar night. Modern historians value these marks because official inventories record what was supplied, whereas damaged pages reveal what was actually read. The little library therefore offers more than a list of Victorian titles: it exposes the distance between institutional ideas about improving people and the imaginative company people seek when ordinary social life disappears.",questions:[["Why do historians value the damaged books?","Purpose",["They are financially valuable","They show actual reading habits","They contain weather measurements","They prove the inventory was false"],"b","Damaged pages, repairs and annotations show what the crew actually read, unlike the official inventory, which only records what was supplied."],["What had the committee incorrectly assumed?","Inference",["Scientists disliked all books","Instructional reading would dominate","Travel writing was unavailable","Manuals would be useless"],"b","The selection reflected an overly serious idea of readers' needs."],["The phrase ‘imaginative company’ refers to","Implied meaning",["fiction providing social and emotional escape","visitors arriving at the station","the book committee","scientific collaboration"],"a","Stories substituted for absent ordinary society."],["Which contrast structures the passage?","Main idea",["Old books versus new books","What institutions supplied versus what readers valued","Science versus inaccurate diaries","Arctic weather versus travel"],"b","The inventory and evidence of use tell different stories."],["The tone is best described as","Author's attitude",["amused but analytical","furious and accusatory","sentimental and uncritical","strictly instructional"],"a","The writer notes the mismatch with restrained humour."]]},
{id:"repair-culture",passage:"At a monthly repair café in Brenton, visitors bring lamps, radios and small appliances that shops have declared uneconomical to fix. The volunteers do not promise success. Their first rule is that owners must sit beside the repairer, observe the diagnosis and, where safe, handle the tools. This slows the queue, yet organisers argue that speed is the wrong measure. A repaired toaster prevents one purchase; a visitor who learns to replace a cable may prevent several. Critics note that such events cannot compensate for products sealed with glue or for missing spare parts. The organisers agree, which is why they record failed repairs and send the evidence to manufacturers. The café is thus neither a nostalgic workshop nor a complete solution to electronic waste. It is a place where practical knowledge becomes a form of consumer pressure.",questions:[["Why must owners participate?","Detail",["To reduce insurance costs","To acquire reusable repair knowledge","To entertain volunteers","To prove ownership"],"b","Learning extends the effect beyond one object."],["Why is speed called the wrong measure?","Inference",["Queues are always short","Longer repairs are cheaper","Education creates longer-term benefits","Volunteers dislike efficiency"],"c","The organisers value future independent repairs."],["What is the purpose of recording failures?","Purpose",["To discourage visitors","To pressure producers with evidence","To advertise new products","To rank volunteers"],"b","Failures document design barriers."],["‘Compensate for’ is closest to","Vocabulary in context",["fully make up for","hide","calculate","pay a volunteer for"],"a","The café cannot overcome every structural obstacle."],["Which description best captures the café?","Main idea",["A complete waste solution","A commercial repair shop","A learning project with wider advocacy aims","A museum of old electronics"],"c","It combines practical education and pressure for change."]]},
{id:"street-dance",passage:"When a city museum announced an exhibition on street dance, its first plan placed costumes behind glass and presented a timeline written by curators. Local dancers objected that the display treated a living practice as if it had ended. They proposed an open floor, weekly battles and video booths where visitors could add accounts of places important to them. Some curators worried that changing contributions would weaken scholarly authority and make preservation difficult. The final exhibition kept a researched timeline but surrounded it with performances and a digital archive whose entries were reviewed rather than frozen. Attendance exceeded expectations, although the most revealing result came from visitor interviews: many teenagers described themselves not as an audience but as temporary contributors. The museum had not surrendered expertise; it had made expertise answerable to the community whose culture it interpreted.",questions:[["What caused the initial disagreement?","Detail",["The costumes were inaccurate","Dancers saw the culture as living rather than finished","Teenagers opposed performances","Curators refused to preserve videos"],"b","The static display implied that the practice belonged only to the past."],["What does ‘reviewed rather than frozen’ imply?","Implied meaning",["Entries could develop under quality control","All videos were deleted","Only curators could contribute","The archive was temporary"],"a","The archive remained dynamic but moderated."],["What most impressed the writer?","Inference",["Ticket income","The number of costumes","Visitors becoming contributors","The removal of the timeline"],"c","The visitor interviews are called the most revealing result."],["The final sentence suggests expertise should be","Author's attitude",["abandoned","isolated from public opinion","responsible to represented communities","limited to digital work"],"c","Professional knowledge remains but becomes accountable."],["The text primarily illustrates","Main idea",["why museums should avoid youth culture","how participation can coexist with scholarship","why performance cannot be archived","how timelines increase attendance"],"b","The compromise preserves research and living participation."]]},
{id:"quiet-maps",passage:"Tourist maps usually amplify landmarks: towers, museums and crowded viewpoints. A project in Lydon mapped something less visible—the places where residents could find relative quiet. Volunteers recorded sound at different hours, but they also wrote short descriptions, since a low reading did not always feel peaceful. A silent underpass could feel threatening, while a garden containing birdsong and distant conversation felt restorative despite registering more decibels. The resulting map avoided naming a single ‘quietest’ place. Instead, users could filter for shelter, natural sound, accessibility or distance from traffic. The project’s designers argue that this complexity is a strength. By refusing to reduce experience to one number, the map shows that useful evidence may combine measurement with carefully collected judgement.",questions:[["Why were written descriptions added?","Detail",["Sound meters were broken","Low volume did not necessarily mean comfort","Residents disliked maps","Birdsong could not be measured"],"b","Perceived peacefulness depended on context."],["The underpass and garden examples show that","Inference",["all gardens are loud","safety and sound quality affect experience","decibels are never useful","conversation prevents restoration"],"b","The same numerical scale cannot capture the whole experience."],["‘Amplify’ most nearly means","Vocabulary in context",["make more prominent","make physically louder","measure inaccurately","remove from view"],"a","Ordinary tourist maps foreground landmarks."],["Why is there no single quietest place?","Purpose",["The team lost its results","Quiet has several relevant dimensions","Users cannot understand rankings","Traffic changes every minute"],"b","Filters reflect different needs and perceptions."],["What principle does the author support?","Main idea",["Judgement should replace evidence","Good evidence can integrate numbers and experience","Only residents should make maps","Tourists prefer silence"],"b","The project combines measurement with systematic judgement."]]},
{id:"seed-voyage",passage:"During long nineteenth-century sea voyages, botanical collectors often packed seeds in paper, only to find that salt air and damp had destroyed them before arrival. The invention of a glazed travelling case changed the odds. Plants sealed inside recycled their own moisture and were protected from spray, although they still needed light and careful positioning on deck. The cases helped move economically valuable crops between colonies, but they also transported ornamental species that later escaped cultivation and disrupted local ecosystems. Celebratory accounts call the case a triumph of simple engineering. That description is technically fair but historically incomplete: an object can solve the problem it was designed for while enlarging consequences its inventor never considered.",questions:[["What problem did the glazed case solve?","Detail",["A shortage of paper","Damage during sea transport","Lack of ornamental plants","Poor ship navigation"],"b","Before the glazed case, salt air and damp destroyed seeds during sea voyages; the case protected plants from damp and spray."],["Why did cases still need careful positioning?","Detail",["They required access to light","They were too valuable to display","Seeds needed salt water","Crews used them as windows"],"a","The text says the cases protected plants from damp and spray, but they still needed light and careful positioning on deck. The correct option paraphrases this remaining condition: access to light."],["What unintended effect is described?","Inference",["Crops became less valuable","Some imported species harmed ecosystems","Collectors stopped travelling","Cases used too much water"],"b","The text says ornamental species transported in the cases later escaped cultivation and disrupted local ecosystems."],["The author calls celebratory accounts incomplete because they","Author's attitude",["deny the engineering worked","ignore wider historical consequences","focus on ecology only","misidentify the inventor"],"b","Technical success is only part of the story."],["What broader idea concludes the text?","Main idea",["Simple inventions have simple effects","Design success can coexist with unforeseen harm","Plant transport should end","Inventors are responsible for every future event"],"b","A solution may generate consequences beyond its intended scope."]]}
