{id:"synthetic-voices",passage:"As synthetic voices become harder to distinguish from recorded speech, public debate has concentrated on fraud. The danger is real, but an exclusive focus on detection overlooks a subtler question: when is a voice merely a tool, and when does it represent a person? A museum may reconstruct a historical figure’s speech from written records, making an archive more accessible while also giving disputed words an aura of certainty. An actor may license a vocal model for translations yet discover that intonation alters the character originally performed. Some ethicists therefore argue that consent should concern not only whether a voice may be reproduced, but the contexts, emotions and claims it may be made to express. Authentication can tell listeners that audio was generated; it cannot decide whether the generation was legitimate.",questions:[["What limitation of the fraud debate does the writer identify?","Main idea",["Detection systems are inexpensive","It neglects questions of representation and legitimacy","Museums never use synthetic speech","Recorded speech is always authentic"],"b","The passage moves beyond detection to ethical representation."],["Why might reconstructed museum speech mislead?","Inference",["Visitors dislike archives","It can make uncertain wording sound authoritative","Historical figures spoke quietly","Written records contain no language"],"b","A convincing voice may conceal uncertainty in the source."],["What does ‘aura of certainty’ imply?","Vocabulary in context",["an unjustified impression of confidence","a technical audio fault","a legal form of consent","an emotional performance"],"a","The reconstruction can sound more definite than the evidence warrants."],["The actor example chiefly demonstrates that","Purpose",["translation is impossible","permission to copy a voice may not cover every performance choice","actors own every language","intonation has no effect"],"b","Consent needs to address contexts and expressive uses."],["The final sentence distinguishes between","Implied meaning",["human and historical voices","technical disclosure and ethical justification","actors and museums","speech and writing"],"b","Labelling generated audio does not settle whether its use is right."]]},
{id:"ethical-ruins",passage:"Digital reconstruction allows visitors to walk through buildings that survive only as foundations. Its apparent precision, however, can disguise the number of choices involved. Archaeological evidence may establish the width of a wall but not its colour; a written source may describe splendour without explaining whose labour produced it. Designers often fill such gaps because an incomplete model feels unsatisfying. Yet visual fluency can turn a hypothesis into an apparent fact. A more responsible reconstruction might display several alternatives, reveal confidence levels or allow users to switch off speculative layers. This would make the experience less seamless, but perhaps more historically honest. The aim should not be to abolish imagination—without it no reconstruction is possible—but to prevent imagination from quietly borrowing the authority of evidence.",questions:[["What central risk does the passage identify?","Main idea",["Digital models are too expensive","Smooth visual detail can conceal speculation","Archaeology provides no measurements","Visitors reject complete models"],"b","The concern is unmarked interpretation appearing factual."],["Why do designers fill evidential gaps?","Inference",["Software requires bright colours","Users may find incompleteness frustrating","Written sources are always false","Several alternatives are illegal"],"b","A seamless experience encourages unsupported completion."],["‘Visual fluency’ refers to the model’s","Vocabulary in context",["linguistic accuracy","convincing and coherent appearance","low resolution","ability to translate inscriptions"],"b","A polished model feels naturally credible."],["Which solution does the author favour?","Author's position",["Removing all reconstruction","Making uncertainty visible","Choosing the most attractive hypothesis","Using written sources only"],"b","Alternatives and confidence levels expose uncertainty."],["The final metaphor suggests imagination can","Implied meaning",["gain undeserved credibility from evidence","replace artistic skill","make ruins physically stable","eliminate historical debate"],"a","Speculation can inherit evidence's authority if unmarked."]]},
{id:"productive-friction",passage:"Education platforms often promise to remove friction: one click to enrol, automatic hints, immediate correction. Some obstacles plainly waste attention, yet researchers studying durable learning distinguish inconvenience from intellectual resistance. A difficult retrieval attempt may feel inefficient while strengthening memory; waiting before receiving feedback can force a learner to inspect the reasoning behind an answer. This does not justify confusion for its own sake. Poor instructions and inaccessible interfaces consume effort without deepening understanding. The design problem is therefore diagnostic: which difficulty belongs to the idea being learned, and which has been accidentally created by the system? A platform that treats every pause as failure may become pleasant to use while quietly removing the conditions under which independent judgement develops.",questions:[["What distinction is central to the passage?","Main idea",["Online and classroom learning","Productive difficulty and needless obstruction","Memory and intelligence","Hints and correction"],"b","Not every form of friction has the same educational value."],["Why can delayed feedback help?","Inference",["It makes courses cheaper","It prompts examination of reasoning","It guarantees correct answers","It reduces retrieval"],"b","The learner must consider how the answer was reached."],["What does ‘diagnostic’ imply about design?","Vocabulary in context",["Designers must identify the source and function of difficulty","Platforms require medical testing","All pauses should be measured","Interfaces should become harder"],"a","The origin of effort determines whether it is useful."],["The author’s attitude to friction-free platforms is","Author's attitude",["entirely enthusiastic","qualified and sceptical","nostalgic","unconcerned"],"b","Convenience is accepted but its educational cost is questioned."],["What is implied by the final sentence?","Implied meaning",["Enjoyable systems always teach well","Ease of use can mask weaker intellectual development","Independent judgement requires bad instructions","Every learner dislikes pauses"],"b","Pleasant interaction is not identical to durable learning."]]},
{id:"news-corrections",passage:"News organisations usually place corrections beneath the original article, preserving transparency but reaching only readers who return. Experiments with more visible corrections produce an awkward result: repeated exposure to a false claim, even in order to deny it, can make the claim feel familiar, and familiarity is sometimes mistaken for truth. This has led some editors to favour ‘truth-first’ corrections that begin with verified information, mention the falsehood once and explain why it fails. Critics worry that such careful framing looks evasive, especially when an error was the publication’s own. The disagreement is not between accuracy and deception. It concerns two responsibilities that can pull apart: documenting precisely what went wrong and reducing the chance that the error will continue to shape belief.",questions:[["Why may conventional corrections fail?","Inference",["They are always inaccurate","Few readers return and repetition can reinforce familiarity","Editors refuse to mention errors","Verified facts are unavailable"],"b","Both limited reach and familiarity effects are identified."],["What is a truth-first correction?","Detail",["A correction that hides the original article","One that foregrounds verified information before addressing the false claim","A legal apology","A repeated headline"],"b","It starts from the supported account."],["Why might critics call this framing evasive?","Author's position",["It may seem to soften responsibility for the original error","It contains too many facts","It reaches returning readers","It removes all explanation"],"a","Careful wording may appear to avoid direct admission."],["‘Pull apart’ most nearly means","Vocabulary in context",["become physically separated","create competing demands","prove identical","cancel permanently"],"b","The two legitimate responsibilities may conflict."],["The passage presents the issue as","Main idea",["a choice between truth and lies","a tension between transparency and limiting misinformation","proof that corrections are useless","a criticism of all editors"],"b","The final paragraph explicitly defines this tension."]]},
{id:"moral-licensing",passage:"After making an environmentally responsible choice, people sometimes become more willing to permit themselves a less responsible one—a pattern called moral licensing. Someone who cycles to work may feel entitled to take an unnecessary flight, although the two actions differ vastly in impact. The concept is tempting because it explains inconsistency with a neat psychological mechanism. Evidence, however, is mixed. In some studies an initial good action strengthens a person’s environmental identity and encourages consistency; in others it functions like credit to be spent. Context appears decisive: public praise, the size of the sacrifice and whether the action is framed as ‘who I am’ or merely ‘what I did’ can alter what follows. Moral licensing is therefore better treated as a possibility to investigate than as a universal excuse for contradictory behaviour.",questions:[["Why is moral licensing an attractive explanation?","Inference",["It makes inconsistency seem psychologically coherent","It proves cycling is ineffective","It measures flight emissions","It applies only to public actions"],"a","It offers a simple mechanism for contradictory choices."],["What does the evidence show?","Detail",["Good actions always licence bad ones","Initial behaviour can produce either consistency or compensation","Identity has no effect","Sacrifice guarantees consistency"],"b","Studies point in opposing directions."],["The metaphor ‘credit to be spent’ suggests a person may","Implied meaning",["expect payment for cycling","use one good act to justify a later harmful act","calculate emissions accurately","avoid public praise"],"b","The earlier act is treated as moral permission."],["What is the author’s position?","Author's attitude",["The theory explains all inconsistency","The pattern is conditional rather than universal","The research should be abandoned","Environmental identity is fictional"],"b","The conclusion explicitly resists universal application."],["Which factor is NOT mentioned as affecting later behaviour?","Detail",["Public recognition","Perceived sacrifice","Identity framing","The person's age"],"d","Age is not discussed."]]},
{id:"slow-science",passage:"Calls for ‘slow science’ are sometimes caricatured as resistance to productivity. Their advocates make a narrower claim: research systems that reward frequent publication can encourage scholars to divide findings into minimal papers, avoid risky long-term questions and treat time spent checking another team’s work as professionally invisible. Slowness itself guarantees nothing; a badly designed project does not improve by lasting longer. The relevant issue is whether institutions protect activities whose value appears late—replication, careful dataset construction and relationships with communities being studied. These activities can delay an individual publication while increasing the reliability of an entire field. The slogan is useful only if it redirects attention from the speed of visible outputs to the less visible conditions under which trustworthy knowledge is produced.",questions:[["What misconception about slow science is rejected?","Main idea",["It opposes all productivity","It values replication","It concerns institutions","It protects communities"],"a","The opening calls this interpretation a caricature."],["Why may frequent-publication incentives be harmful?","Inference",["They make papers too long","They discourage risky and verification work","They eliminate datasets","They require community research"],"b","Researchers may optimise visible output at the expense of reliability."],["What does ‘professionally invisible’ imply?","Vocabulary in context",["Secret research is illegal","Important work may receive little career recognition","Other teams cannot see results","Replication uses no documents"],"b","The incentive system overlooks that contribution."],["Why does the writer say slowness guarantees nothing?","Purpose",["To reject the movement entirely","To prevent duration itself becoming a false measure of quality","To recommend shorter studies","To criticise datasets"],"b","Time is valuable only when it enables worthwhile practices."],["The author ultimately supports evaluating research by","Author's position",["number of papers","speed alone","conditions that produce dependable knowledge","length of community meetings"],"c","Trustworthiness rather than visible speed is the proposed standard."]]}
